      en: "The Port of Toamasina, beyond its economic dominance, is a defining structural landmark of Madagascar's eastern coast. The vast, heavily industrialized complex dominates the city's waterfront with a skyline of massive cargo cranes, towering grain silos, and stacked shipping containers. The port's defining feature is its extensive breakwater (digue), which juts out into the rough Indian Ocean to protect the deep-water anchorages from severe cyclones. Originally developed in the late 19th century, the landscape is currently undergoing a massive $400 million physical expansion, dramatically extending the concrete piers further into the sea. The constant, illuminated activity of the port at night forms the industrial heartbeat of the nation. Geografi K7 — industrial landmarks and maritime infrastructure."
